// NEGATIVE TEST -- a `for` loop variable may not take a top-level item's name
// (N3-09, N3-10).
//
// The same fresh-binder question as tests/reject/pattern_binder_shadows_item.pd,
// one construct over: `for LIMIT in 0..3` declares a NEW `LIMIT` for the body,
// so the item is invisible inside the loop and the body reads 0, 1, 2 instead.
// Measured before this refusal: printed 0 1 2 with no diagnostic.
//
// FIVE BINDER POSITIONS ask the same question through one function
// (`refuse_global_shadow` in src/typeck/mod.rs): a `let`, a parameter, a
// pattern binder, an `@` binding, and this `for` header. It had TWO call sites
// -- the sentence here used to say "three" while listing four and omitting the
// `@` form, which is its own small lesson about counting in prose -- and the
// three binder positions it never saw were all silent, which is why the rule
// looked implemented while most of it was not.
const LIMIT: i64 = 10;

fn main() {
    for LIMIT in 0..3 {
        print_int(LIMIT);
    }
}
